 "note": "The Table 1 CRAM recipe on TRM's stored augmented Sudoku-Extreme training rows, the same\nrows that TRM and EBR train on, with CRAM's per-update digit relabeling turned off (FullConfig.aug = False).\n\n  python data_efficiency.py fit --k 1000 --seed 0                    # one cold fit in this process, then 7 warm fits\n  python data_efficiency.py search --k 1000 --seed 0 --data screen   # ED1's 512 dev10k puzzles\n  python data_efficiency.py search --k 1000 --seed 0 --data test     # the 412,786 unseen test puzzles, once\n  python data_efficiency.py export                                   # ../results/sudoku-data-eff-shared.json\n\nData: seed s, k < 1,000: runs/trm/data/sudoku-smalldata/s<s>-n<k>/train (baselines/trm_smalldata.py: the first k groups of\ndefault_rng(s).permutation(1000) over TRM's 1,000-puzzle set, each the original and its 1,000 stored copies);\nk = 1,000: TRM's set runs/trm/data/sudoku-extreme-1k-aug-1000/train for every seed.\nTRM tokens: 0 pad, 1 blank, digit d (1-9) as d + 1; so CRAM's digit (0-8) is label - 2 and a free cell is input == 1.\nk = 3,000 and 10,000: runs/trm/data/sudoku-smalldata/s<s>-n<k> (the 1,000-puzzle set plus new puzzles, same builder).\nEverything after the fit (tau, the search, its checkpointing) is data_efficiency_common's, run with CRAM_E_ROOT=DE1.\nAbove BIG rows the full-batch gradient and prepare()'s training-set statistics are summed over chunks of CHUNK rows: the\nsame updates and the same tau (a maximum) up to the floating-point summation order, in less GPU memory (one full batch\nof 1,001,000 rows peaks at 11.9 GiB).\nDE_VARIANTS=V keeps the original and the first V of the 1,000 stored copies of each group\n(rows 0..V of every 1,001-row group), so the training set is a row subset of the same files; outputs go to\nruns/CRAM-E/DE2-v<V>. DE_VARIANTS unset (1,000) is DE1.\nDimension names: q puzzles, n = 81 cells.\n",
